; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Flood Fill by Recursion
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     recursion, flood fill, grids, arrays, paint bucket
; SUMMARY:  Shows how a paint bucket tool works: a cell paints itself and then asks its four neighbors to do the same, spreading until it meets walls.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   The screen is a grid of square cells. Some cells are walls: a ring
;   and a square box inside it. FLOOD is the paint bucket. Asked to
;   paint a cell, it gives up if the cell is off the grid, is a wall,
;   or is already painted. Otherwise it paints the cell and then calls
;   FLOOD on the cells above, to the right, below and to the left. The
;   color leaks outward like water until walls stop it.
;   The first flood starts between the ring and the box, so it paints a
;   doughnut and never gets into the box. A second flood fills the box.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run and watch the paint spread. The pattern it makes shows
;      the order of the recursive calls: it runs up in a straight line
;      first, because "up" is tried first.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the line  paint.cell :row :col :color
;      and run. Press F5 a few times, then open the Call Stack. Each
;      FLOOD there is a cell still waiting to try its other neighbors.
;   3. Add a watch for  :painted  and continue. The deepest the stack
;      gets is printed at the end.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * four-way recursion on a grid
;   * the three stop rules: off the grid, a wall, already done
;   * MDARRAY and MDITEM for a two dimensional map
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Change the order of the four FLOOD calls and see the new pattern.
;   * Add the four diagonal neighbors. Can the paint now slip between
;     wall cells that only touch at their corners?
;   * Make a gap in the box (change one wall cell) and run again.
; ============================================================

make "rows 24
make "cols 32
make "cell 22
make "painted 0
make "depth 0
make "deepest 0

; Mark the walls. The middle of the grid is between cells, at column
; 16.5 and row 12.5. Cells about 10.5 away from it form a ring. Cells
; that are 4.5 away across OR up and down (whichever is bigger) form
; the sides of a square box.
to build.walls
  make "grid mdarray (list :rows :cols)
  for [r 1 :rows] [
    for [c 1 :cols] [
      localmake "dx :c - 16.5
      localmake "dy :r - 12.5
      localmake "d sqrt (:dx * :dx + :dy * :dy)
      ifelse or (abs (:d - 10.5)) < 0.75 (max abs :dx abs :dy) = 4.5 [
        mdsetitem (list :r :c) :grid "wall
        paint.cell :r :c [40 40 60]
      ] [
        mdsetitem (list :r :c) :grid "empty
      ]
    ]
  ]
end

to max :a :b
  ifelse :a > :b [output :a] [output :b]
end

to flood :row :col :color
  ; the three stop rules
  if or :row < 1 :row > :rows [stop]
  if or :col < 1 :col > :cols [stop]
  if not equalp mditem (list :row :col) :grid "empty [stop]
  mdsetitem (list :row :col) :grid "painted
  make "painted :painted + 1
  make "depth :depth + 1
  if :depth > :deepest [make "deepest :depth]
  paint.cell :row :col :color
  wait 1
  flood :row - 1 :col :color
  flood :row :col + 1 :color
  flood :row + 1 :col :color
  flood :row :col - 1 :color
  make "depth :depth - 1
end

; Paint one cell with thick strokes. Strokes always land on top of the
; picture, which is what we want when coloring cells one by one.
to paint.cell :row :col :color
  localmake "x (:col - 1) * :cell - :cols * :cell / 2
  localmake "y :rows * :cell / 2 - (:row - 1) * :cell
  setpencolor :color setpensize 5
  for [k 3 20 4] [
    penup setxy :x + 3 :y - :k
    pendown setxy :x + :cell - 3 :y - :k
  ]
  penup
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [235 235 225]
build.walls
flood 12 23 [70 150 230]
print (sentence [The doughnut took] :painted [cells, and the stack was] :deepest [calls deep at most.])
make "painted 0
make "deepest 0
flood 12 16 [240 120 60]
print (sentence [The box took] :painted [cells, and the stack was] :deepest [calls deep at most.])
